# Tutorial 07 — Variables and Conditions
# How to track game state, branch dialogue, and react to player choices.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_07_variables
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: This is where your story stops being linear and starts being {i}yours{/i}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Variables let you remember things — what the player chose, how many times they visited a place, whether they're on a good path or a bad one.
Lavender: Combined with conditions, they let you write stories that react to the player.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What do you want to learn?
> Setting and reading variables -> section_set
> Incrementing and math -> section_math
> If / elif / else conditions -> section_conditions
> Variables with choices -> section_choices_vars
> Naming conventions -> section_naming
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Setting Variables ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_set
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The {b}[set]{/b} command creates a variable and gives it a value.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set courage 0]{/color}
Lavender: Now there's a variable called "courage" with the value 0. It persists for the entire playthrough.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can set it to any number, or to a string:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set player_name "Alex"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set chapter 1]{/color}
Lavender: Variables are global — once set, any script file can read or change them.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Think of them as the game's memory. "Did the player help the cat?" — store a variable. "Which ending did they unlock?" — store a variable.
> Next: Math -> section_math
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Math Operations ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_math
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: You don't always want to set a specific value. Sometimes you just want to nudge it.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc courage]{/color}
Lavender: That adds 1 to courage. If it was 0, now it's 1.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: There's also {b}[dec]{/b} to subtract:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[dec courage]{/color}
Lavender: And you can set a variable to the result of math:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set total_score courage + kindness]{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: These are great for tracking cumulative choices. Every time the player does something brave, {b}[inc courage]{/b}. At the end, check how high it got.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep the math simple — if your variable logic needs a spreadsheet to understand, it's too complex for a VN script.
> Next: Conditions -> section_conditions
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Conditions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_conditions
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Conditions let you show different content based on variable values. Here's the basic pattern:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if courage >= 3]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You've been really brave.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[elif courage >= 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You showed some courage.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[else]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Maybe next time you'll be braver.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[if]{/b} checks the condition. {b}[elif]{/b} checks another if the first was false. {b}[else]{/b} catches everything else.
Lavender: Always close with {b}[endif]{/b} — the engine needs to know where the block ends.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Operators you can use: {b}=={/b} (equal), {b}!={/b} (not equal), {b}>={/b}, {b}<={/b}, {b}>{/b}, {b}<{/b}.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: You can also check string variables:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if player_name == "Alex"]{/color}
> Next: Variables with choices -> section_choices_vars
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Variables with Choices ──────────────────────────────────────

@label section_choices_vars
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The real power comes from combining variables with player choices. Watch this:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: The kitten is stuck in the tree. What do you do?{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Climb up and help -> choice_climb{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Walk away -> choice_walk{/color}
Lavender: Then at each label:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label choice_climb{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc courage]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc kindness]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You saved the kitten!{/color}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Now later in the story, you can check those values and react differently.
Lavender: Every choice can quietly adjust the numbers behind the scenes. The player never sees the variables — they just see a story that feels responsive.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: This is how route systems, affection meters, and branching endings work in visual novels.
> Next: Naming -> section_naming
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Naming Conventions ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_naming
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A word on naming. Variables pile up fast, so stay consistent.
Lavender: Good names:
narrator: {b}courage{/b}, {b}kindness{/b}, {b}chapter{/b}, {b}met_librarian{/b}, {b}route_active{/b}
Lavender: Bad names:
narrator: {b}x{/b}, {b}temp{/b}, {b}flag1{/b}, {b}thing{/b}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use snake_case, keep names descriptive, and prefix related variables:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1_courage, ch1_kindness, ch2_trust{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Future-you will appreciate it when there are fifty variables and you need to find one.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Variables and conditions cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}[set name value]{/b} — create or overwrite a variable.
narrator: {b}[inc name]{/b} — add 1 to a numeric variable.
narrator: {b}[dec name]{/b} — subtract 1 from a numeric variable.
narrator: {b}[if condition]{/b} — start a conditional block.
narrator: {b}[elif condition]{/b} — additional check if previous was false.
narrator: {b}[else]{/b} — fallback if all checks fail.
narrator: {b}[endif]{/b} — close the conditional block.
narrator: {b}Operators:{/b} ==, !=, >=, <=, >, <
narrator: {b}Values:{/b} numbers or quoted strings.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep variable names descriptive and your condition blocks short. If an [if] has ten [elif]s, consider restructuring.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[set courage 0]
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's see variables in action. I'll ask you a few questions and track your answers.
Lavender: Ready?
> Let's go -> practice_q1

@label practice_q1
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A stranger drops their wallet on the sidewalk. What do you do?
> Pick it up and return it -> practice_q1_kind
> Keep walking -> practice_q1_skip

@label practice_q1_kind
[inc courage]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That was nice of you.
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q1_skip
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Fair enough. Not everyone stops.
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q2
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: There's a shortcut through a dark alley. The safe path takes ten minutes longer.
> Take the alley -> practice_q2_brave
> Go the safe way -> practice_q2_safe

@label practice_q2_brave
[inc courage]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Gutsy move.
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_q2_safe
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Smart. No sense in taking risks.
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_result
[show lavender center talking]
[if courage >= 2]
Lavender: Courage: 2. You're bold — the kind of player who charges into the unknown.
[elif courage == 1]
Lavender: Courage: 1. A mix of caution and heart. Very human.
[else]
Lavender: Courage: 0. You play it safe. Nothing wrong with that — some stories reward patience.
[endif]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: See how that worked? Two simple [inc] commands and one [if] block, and the ending feels personal.
Lavender: That's the whole trick — small tracking, big payoff.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
